[Unit]
Description=LitClock Shutdown Splash Screen
Documentation=https://github.com/kapoorankush/litclock
DefaultDependencies=no
# Conflicts= triggers our STOP (and ExecStop) when systemd isolates to any of
# the four shutdown targets.
#
# Do NOT add litclock.service or litclock.timer here. Conflicts= is bidirectional
# and these units stay active during normal operation:
#   * litclock.service is timer-fired every minute. Bidirectional Conflicts would
#     fire shutdown-splash.sh on every minute tick.
#   * litclock.timer is enabled and active forever. Bidirectional Conflicts would
#     mean at boot, whichever starts first stops the other — either the splash
#     paints during boot, or the timer never fires.
# Both were tried and reverted; ordering via Before= below is the safe shape.
Conflicts=shutdown.target reboot.target halt.target poweroff.target
# Before= controls ordering. In shutdown direction Before= reverses to mean
# "our stop runs after theirs". The four shutdown targets keep our ExecStop
# ordered before each shutdown path completes (pre-PR state). Adding
# litclock.service (litclock-dev#271) makes ExecStop wait until the mid-render clock
# service — which has its own default Conflicts=shutdown.target, so it IS
# being stopped in the same shutdown transaction — has fully stopped and
# released the SPI bus. Without it, systemd is free to stop both units
# concurrently and the SPI/GPIO race returns.
#
# Known narrow edge case (P3 follow-up): if `sudo reboot` lands in the ~1s
# window where litclock.timer was about to enqueue a fresh litclock.service
# start, the queued start can land after our ExecStop. Before=litclock.service
# only orders against already-active instances. A proper fix needs systemd
# semantics that aren't crisp in the docs (one-way Before=litclock.timer may
# or may not cancel queued service starts) and would benefit from a unit
# redesign. See follow-up issue.
#
# An earlier draft used Before=final.target as a single-line cover for all
# four shutdown paths AND as an attempted litclock-dev#268 fix. Codex review pointed out
# Before=final.target only constrains when our stop must *finish*, not when
# it *starts* — Conflicts= queues the stop immediately at the beginning of
# the shutdown transaction either way. litclock-dev#268 needs a proper late-shutdown-hook
# design, not a one-liner. Tracked separately.
#
# litclock-dev#856: litclock-splash.service joins the same list, for the same
# reason and with one extra consequence.
#
#   STOP DIRECTION (the fix). Before= reverses on shutdown, so our ExecStop
#   now runs only after the boot splash's stop job has COMPLETED. That
#   matters because litclock-splash.service is a Type=oneshot whose
#   ExecStart (boot-splash.sh -> `timeout 20 python src/eink_display.py`)
#   holds GPIO17/SPI for ~11s of every boot. Stopping a oneshot that is
#   still in its start state does not run an ExecStop (there is none here)
#   — it SIGTERMs the whole control group and the stop job finishes when
#   that cgroup drains, i.e. when the kernel closes the gpiochip handle.
#   Before this line, a `sudo reboot` inside the splash window left the
#   splash's stop and our ExecStop unordered siblings — sharing the
#   successor litclock.service does NOT serialise them — and
#   shutdown-splash.sh raced the boot splash for the panel, died with the
#   same `KeyError: PinInfo(... 'GPIO17' ...)` litclock-dev#832 removed from
#   the boot side, swallowed it on its `|| true` tail, and the boot splash
#   rode across the power-off. Outside that window the splash has already
#   exited and its stop job is a no-op, so this edge costs nothing.
#
#   NOT A PROOF OF RELEASE, though (litclock-dev#856 review). systemd 252 can finish
#   a stop in a FAILED state with processes still present once the SIGKILL
#   timeout expires, so a python wedged in uninterruptible kernel I/O on the
#   SPI transfer can outlive its own stop job and still hold the panel.
#   Ordering buys the ordinary case, which is all of them we have seen; it
#   does not make the race impossible. Residual, not a regression.
#
#   PRECONDITION, load-bearing and easy to delete by accident: this edge
#   only does anything while litclock-splash.service HAS a stop job in the
#   shutdown transaction, which it has solely because its default
#   dependencies give it an implicit Conflicts=shutdown.target. Adding
#   DefaultDependencies=no to the splash — which reads like consistency with
#   the line four above this one — drops it out of the transaction and
#   silently turns this Before= into a no-op. Same argument the litclock-dev#271
#   paragraph makes for litclock.service. Pinned by
#   test_splash_is_in_the_shutdown_transaction_at_all.
#
#   START DIRECTION (a second, smaller hole closed). Before= also means our
#   /bin/true start job completes before the splash's start job begins.
#   Both units are WantedBy=multi-user.target and were previously unordered
#   against each other, so a reboot during the splash window could find US
#   still activating — and a oneshot stopped out of its start state never
#   reaches ExecStop, i.e. no shutdown paint at all. Now we are guaranteed
#   active for the whole splash window. No boot cost: we are
#   DefaultDependencies=no/After=local-fs.target and the splash carries the
#   implicit After=basic.target, so we were already ready long before it.
#
#   NO CYCLE. Every edge touching these units, start direction:
#     local-fs.target        -> litclock-shutdown          (After=, ours)
#     litclock-shutdown      -> litclock-splash            (Before=, litclock-dev#856)
#     litclock-shutdown      -> litclock.service           (Before=, litclock-dev#271)
#     litclock-shutdown      -> {shutdown,reboot,halt,poweroff}.target
#     local-fs -> sysinit.target -> basic.target -> litclock-splash
#                                                  (splash's After= + implicit)
#     litclock-splash        -> litclock.service           (After=, litclock-dev#832)
#     litclock-splash        -> litclock-firstboot.service (firstboot's After=,
#                               pre-existing; now transitively behind us too)
#     litclock-splash        -> shutdown.target            (implicit Before=,
#                               from its default dependencies — the
#                               PRECONDITION above, and why it has a stop job)
#   A DAG.
#   The splash's ExecStartPost hand-off (`systemctl start --no-block
#   litclock.service`) is not an edge at all — it is a queued job that waits
#   on the splash's own start job, which is why it must stay --no-block
#   (litclock-dev#832). Ordering NEVER pulls a unit in, so the splash being
#   disabled or absent simply drops the edge, and the single-unit
#   transactions in reset-setup.sh (`systemctl start litclock-shutdown`,
#   `systemctl stop litclock-splash`) are unaffected: an ordering dep to a
#   unit with no job in the transaction is ignored.
#
#   PAIRED WITH A BOUNDED SPLASH STOP. litclock-splash.service had no
#   TimeoutStopSec, i.e. DefaultTimeoutStopSec (90s on Bookworm systemd
#   252) and up to 90s more in the SIGKILL phase, so the splash now carries
#   TimeoutStopSec=10s. Note what that is NOT for: our 30s below is armed
#   when ExecStop is SPAWNED, not when this stop job is enqueued, so a slow
#   predecessor cannot cut the paint short — predecessor budgets are
#   additive reboot LATENCY in front of it. Unbounded, the one-line ordering
#   fix would have traded a rare wrong splash for a rare three-minute
#   reboot, long enough for an impatient owner to pull the power and get no
#   splash at all. (litclock-dev#271's litclock.service comment stated the containment
#   backwards; corrected there.)
Before=shutdown.target reboot.target halt.target poweroff.target litclock.service litclock-splash.service
After=local-fs.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/bin/true
ExecStop=/home/pi/litclock/scripts/shutdown-splash.sh
TimeoutStopSec=30
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
Environment=LITCLOCK_DIR=/home/pi/litclock
User=pi

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
